> API and interpretation

Fitnesses must be finite, nonnegative and not all zero. Overflowing sums use
max-weight scaling; very small weights can still lose their represented CDF
intervals. Target rounding is clamped below the total. These are represented
floating probabilities, with deterministic seeds; no statistical independence or
exact real-valued sampling is claimed. Selected counters exclude model
validation and CDF addition.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Holland, Adaptation in Natural and Artificial Systems|https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262581110/adaptation-in-natural-and-artificial-systems/]

